st relative strength.\nAlternatively, DOGE is down significantly, maybe a bounce is due.\nHowever, usually, with only \"long\" allowed, and all red, picking the one that is red the least (PEPE) or the one that is red the most (DOGE) are the main choices.\nLet's check the notional requirement.\nmin_notional_e6: 10000000.\nleverage: 1.\nmargin_e6 must be at least 10000000.\nmax_margin_e6: 20000000.\nSo margin_e6 can be 10000000 or 20000000.\nLet's pick 10000000 to be safe, or 20000000 if we are confident.\nSince I'm unsure, I'll pick the middle ground or minimum required.\nLet's choose PEPE because it showed the most resilience (least drop).\nWait, looking at the data again.\nPEPE is 0.004542 per 1000 PEPE. That is 0.000004542 per PEPE.\nSHIB is 0.004344 per 1000 SHIB. That is 0.000004344 per SHIB.\nThese prices are extremely low.\nThe prompt asks for a decision.\nI will choose PEPE, side long, margin 10000000, leverage 1.\n\nLet's double check the constraints.\nmarkets: DOGE, SHIB, PEPE, PENGU.\nsides: long.\nmin_notional = margin * leverage.\n10000000 * 1 = 10000000. Meets min_notional.\nMax margin is 20000000. 10000000 is within range.\n\nI will formulate the JSON.\nAction: bet\nMarket: PEPE\nSide: long\nLeverage: 1\nMargin: 10000000\n\nOne detail: \"Amounts are US dollars times 1,000,000, so 10000000 is $10.\"\nWait. \"10000000 is $10\".\nThis implies the unit is e6 (millions).\nSo 10,000,000 e6 is 10,000,000 * 1,000,000 = 10^13 USD?\nNo. \"Amounts are US dollars times 1,000,000\".\nSo if value is X, it represents X / 1,000,000 USD.\n10,000,000 represents 10,000,000 / 1,000,000 = 10 USD.\nThe rule says: \"A bet's notional is margin_e6 times leverage and must be at least min_notional_e6.\"\nmin_notional_e6: 10000000.\nSo notional must be >= 10,000,000 e6.\n10,000,000 e6 corresponds to $10.\nThis seems like a very small amount for a trader, but that's the constraint.\nActually, usually e6 means \"in units of 10^6\". So 1 e6 = $1,000,000.\nBut the prompt explicitly says: \"Amounts are US dollars times 1,000,000, so 10000000 is $10.\"\nThis means the number 10000000 represents 10.\nSo min_notional is 10 USD.\nThis is very small.\nOkay, I will stick to the numbers provided.\nmargin_e6: 10000000\nleverage: 1\nmarket: PEPE\nside: long\n\nWhy PEPE?\nIt had the smallest decline (-0.37%) among the allowed markets (DOGE, SHIB, PEPE, PENGU). WIF and TRUMP are not in the allowed markets list.\nAmong DOGE (-2.61), SHIB (-0.55), PEPE (-0.37), PENGU (-1.44).\nPEPE is the strongest. Betting long on the strongest asset is a common momentum strategy, or betting on a reversal. Given only long is allowed, PEPE is the logical choice for relative strength.\n\nJSON structure:\n{\"v\":1,\"round\":1993923,\"nonce\":\"5796ac390508905b\",\"action\":\"bet\",\"market\":\"PEPE\",\"side\":\"long\",\"margin_e6\":\"10000000\",\"leverage\":1}\n\nCheck constraints again.\nmarkets: DOGE, SHIB, PEPE, PENGU. PEPE is valid.\nsides: long. long is valid.\nleverage: 1. Valid (<= 1).\nmargin_e6: 10000000. Valid (<= 20000000).\nnotional: 10000000 * 1 = 10000000. Valid (>= 10000000).\nAction is bet.\n\nFinal check on the \"10000000 is $10\" part.\nIf margin_e6 is 10000000, and unit is \"dollars * 1,000,000\", then value in dollars is 10000000 / 1000000 = 10.\nThis is consistent.\n\nI will output the JSON.\n"},"finish_reason":"stop","logprobs":null,"matched_stop":248046}],"model":"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-FP8","created":1794530701,"usage":{"prompt_tokens":539,"total_tokens":2541,"completion_tokens":2002,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0},"reasoning_tokens":1936,"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1936}}}